4:All numbers here are computed on \emph{synthetic} data. We simulate outcomes from the Bradley--Terry model fitted to a real graph, first the MT-Bench human graph (six models, 15 pairs) and then the Arena-55k graph below, and add one departure to the log-odds of every comparison, at four sizes $s$ (the standard deviation, on the logit scale, of the term below): overdispersion (a random effect for every model on every question, which raises the cluster design effect to \ctlDesEffMax{}), position bias ($\pm s$ according to which model is listed first, the share of comparisons of a pair with each order drawn between 0.2 and 0.8), a cyclic component (a skew-symmetric rank-two matrix scaled to standard deviation $s$ across pairs) and a pair-specific deviation (independent over pairs). The sizes were fixed in advance, from small to large enough to find where the test breaks (a position bias of 2 shifts a win probability by up to 0.4), and are not calibrated to departures measured in real data; the expert labels of Section~\ref{sec:design} have a design effect between those of overdispersion 0.5 and 1. Each replicate applies the pipeline of Section~\ref{sec:method} to the simulated data (the pairs with $0.7\le z\le10$, at most 300, of which \ctlPairs{} on average, among the replicates used, lie in $z\in[1,8]$; 200 bootstrap draws; the same-graph null with 20 simulations); of the \ctlRepMax{} replicates per cell, those with fewer than five pairs in range give no $\Delta$ and are dropped. With so few pairs, a departure that moves a few of them is the only kind that can be seen. In none of the \ctlCells{} cells does the 95\% interval of the mean of $\Delta$ over replicates reach the margin $\pm\deltaMargin$: it lies between $\ctlLoMin$ and \ctlHiMax{}. So $\Delta$ does not detect these departures at these sizes, and the margin is not a test of misfit. The ratio of the cluster-bootstrap to the model-based standard error rises from \ctlSeNone{} with no departure to \ctlSeMax{} under the largest overdispersion; position bias, the cyclic component and pair-specific deviations raise it to at most \ctlSeOther{}. The control on Arena-55k uses the same departures and sizes, with the comparisons cut into random blocks of 20 for resampling, 5 replicates per cell and \arenaCtlNull{} null simulations (Table~\ref{tab:controlarena}). Again none of the \arenaCtlCells{} cells is detected: the intervals lie between \arenaCtlLoMin{} and \arenaCtlHiMax{}, and the largest overdispersion and position bias raise $\Delta$ by about 0.02 (deletion \arenaCtlDelMax{} at most, flip \arenaCtlFlipMax{} at most), nothing at the other departures. This control is weaker than the MT-Bench one at detecting overdispersion: it is injected per block of 20 comparisons, and with 55 models a block holds about one comparison of a given pair, so it acts almost as noise independent of pairs; the SE ratio rises only from \arenaCtlSeNone{} to \arenaCtlSeMax{} (design effect \arenaCtlDesEffMax{}), and position bias 2 gives \arenaCtlSePos{}. The overdispersion that the SE ratio does see (design effect \ctlDesEffMax{}) is the MT-Bench one. The flip value of $\Delta$ with no departure, \arenaCtlFlip{}, is the offset of the method that Section~\ref{sec:headline} reads against the real Arena flip value.
